The claim that $\lim_{k	o\infty}
ullity(A_0^{(k)}) = m-\ell$ (and likewise for $A_2^{(k)}$) is mathematically false. In any instance where $A_0$ is nonsingular (such as in Example 3 of the paper, where $A_0 = PG$ with nonsingular $P$ and $G$), the cyclic reduction recurrence $A_0^{(k+1)} = A_0^{(k)}(A_1^{(k)})^{-1}A_0^{(k)}$ implies by induction that $A_0^{(k)}$ is nonsingular for every $k\ge 0$, whenever cyclic reduction proceeds without breakdown. Therefore, $
ullity(A_0^{(k)}) = 0$ for all $k$, and the limit of this sequence of integers is 0, contradicting $\lim_{k	o\infty}
ullity(A_0^{(k)}) = m-\ell \ge 1$. In the proof, deducing $\limsup_{k	o\infty}
ullity(A_0^{(k)}) \ge m-\ell$ from $\lim_{k	o\infty} A_0^{(k)}W_{G,1} = 0$ is an invalid inference (convergence of $M_k V$ to 0 does not imply that $V$ lies in the exact null space of $M_k$), and deducing $
ullity(A_0^{(k)}) 	o m-\ell$ from roots of $a_k(z)$ converging to 0 conflates singular values converging to 0 with the dimension of the exact algebraic null space.

